@title STORY • HOLLOWAY: UNDER THE LIGHTS
@bg holloway_kitchen_morning
You're Simone Holloway. Head coach. 47. Detroit raised. It's 4:30 a.m. and you're drinking black coffee over a stack of meet results.
Your son Terrence, home from college, stumbles into the kitchen.
Terrence: Ma. It's literally dark outside.
Holloway: That's when champions get up.
Terrence: You've said that every morning since I was five.
Holloway: And it's been true every morning since you were five.
@bg athletic_office_day
@show holloway
The athletic director drops a folder on your desk. Frank Sterling's latest donation. New lights, new timing system, new everything.
Athletic Director: Simone. Mr. Sterling asked, very politely, if London might get "some flexibility" on the lineup.
> [Bitchy] "Tell Mr. Sterling his daughter can earn it like everybody else." -> a1
> [Good] "London gets the same rules as everyone. That's the flexibility." -> a2
> [Bold] "Then he can coach her himself. With his new lights." -> a3
*a1
@add Integrity 3
Athletic Director: (sighing) I'll... phrase that differently.
Holloway: Don't.
@jump a_done
*a2
@add Integrity 2
The AD nods. He knew what you'd say. He just had to ask.
@jump a_done
*a3
@add Integrity 2
Athletic Director: Simone.
Holloway: He can borrow my whistle.
*a_done
@bg track_stadium_morning
@show london
6:04 a.m. London Sterling walks onto the track four minutes after start. She's talking back before her feet hit the rubber.
You were London once. Detroit, 1996. Fastest girl in the state. Mouthiest girl in the state. Your coach made you run until you learned the difference between confidence and attitude.
> [Good] Two extra laps. Same as anybody. -> b1
> [Sweet] Pull her aside later. Tell her about Detroit. -> b2
*b1
@add Tough_Love 2
London: Put it on my dad's tab.
Holloway: Three laps.
You hide your smile behind your clipboard. You always do.
@jump end
*b2
@add London_Bond 3
Holloway: I was you. Exactly you. Mouth and all.
London: (suspicious) What happened?
Holloway: Somebody cared enough to not let me get away with it. So I'm not letting you get away with it.
London doesn't say anything. But she's on time the next day. And the next. Mostly.
*end
Coach Holloway scares the whole team. She also knows every one of their birthdays, their moms' names and what they're afraid of. That's the part she never lets them see.
